@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Protected page";
}

<h1>You are signed in</h1>
<p class="sub">
    This page is protected. Reaching it means the whole authorization code flow completed.
</p>

<div class="card">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">What it took</h2>
    <pre>[Authorize]
public IActionResult Secure() => View();</pre>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        That is the entire protection. When an anonymous request reaches this action the
        authorization middleware issues a challenge, the OpenID Connect handler builds the
        authorization request — <code>state</code>, <code>nonce</code>, <code>code_challenge</code>
        and all — and after sign-in it exchanges the code, validates the ID token against the
        provider's published keys, and writes an encrypted cookie. Then the request is replayed
        here.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Things worth not doing</h2>
    <table>
        <tr>
            <td class="k">Reading the token from a cookie</td>
            <td class="v">
                Use <code>HttpContext.GetArkAccessTokenAsync()</code>. The token lives inside the
                encrypted authentication cookie, not in a readable one, and copying a bearer token
                into an <code>Authorization</code> header on every request is what makes an
                application CSRF-able.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="k">Caching the access token</td>
            <td class="v">
                The cookie handler refreshes it in the background shortly before it expires, so a
                cached copy goes stale and starts returning 401s.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="k">Validating the token yourself</td>
            <td class="v">
                Already done, against the provider's JWKS, with issuer, audience, lifetime and
                <code>nonce</code> all checked. Re-validating by hand is how key rotation breaks.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="k">Adding an action at the callback path</td>
            <td class="v">
                <code>/signin-oidc</code> belongs to the handler. A controller action there
                shadows it and the callback never completes.
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>
</div>

<p><a class="btn ghost" href="/home/profile">See what the sign-in produced</a></p>
